 ],
 "literal": "Listen, may you live, O friend! At night, / the man skilled in lies, accustomed to embracing my body, / a false dream with the appearance of truth having deluded me, rising up eagerly, / I caressed the bed. Like a blue water-lily / flower on which bees land, having withered, / I am alone, surely, pitiable am I!",
 "translation": "Listen, my friend, and may you live long! Last night, a false dream that seemed so real deluded me. It made me feel the embrace of that man so skilled in lies. Eagerly rising up, I stroked the bed only to find it empty. Now, like a blue water-lily crushed by visiting bees, I have withered. Surely, I am all alone, and so pitiable!",
 "modern": "தோழியே! என் சொல்லைக் கேள், நீ டூழி வாழ்க! நேற்றிரவு, பொய் சொல்வதில் வல்லவனான என் தலைவன் என் உடலைத் தழுவுவது போன்ற ஒரு கனவு கண்டேன். உண்மையானது போலத் தோன்றிய அந்தப் பொய்க் கனவு என்னை ஏமாற்றியது. நான் ஆசையோடு விழித்தெழுந்து படுக்கையைத் தடவிப் பார்த்தேன். அங்கே அவன் இல்லை. வண்டுகள் மொய்த்த குவளை மலர் வாடிப் போவது போல என் உடல் வாடிப் போயிற்று. நான் தனியாகவும் மிகவும் இரங்கத்தக்கவளாகவும் ஆகிவிட்டேன்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine (talaivi) is speaking to her close friend (tōḻi) during her separation from her lover. She describes a vivid dream she had during the night where her lover, whom she calls 'the man skilled in lies' due to his unkept promises, returned and embraced her. The dream was so realistic that she woke up in excitement and reached out to touch him on the bed, only to find it empty. This cruel disappointment leaves her completely withered, a state she compares to a blue water-lily (kuvaḷai) that has faded after being repeatedly visited and crushed by bees. The contrast between the warmth of the dream-embrace and the cold reality of the empty bed highlights her deep loneliness and grief in his absence.",
